From 6c86ae989f2c9856ab11416da0bc22689ac638b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:25:35 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: debug engine: signal stops, exact steps over
 syscalls and string loops, batched registers

Signals. Every signal about to be delivered to a thread of the debugged group
stops the group first (event SIGNAL, no tracer involved): get_signal calls the
engine under the signal lock, the engine drops the lock and parks the thread as
a tracer's signal stop would, and the debugger decides, through CONT, whether
the thread gets the signal, another one, or none. A step that delivers a signal
stops at the handler's first instruction (signal_delivered), and a step armed
when a signal is about to be delivered is disarmed first, so the engine's trap
flag is never in a signal frame. This closes the phase 1 risk of a pending
signal during a step.

Steps are one user instruction. A SYSCALL (or INT 0x80) and a repeated string
instruction run to the instruction after them under a temporary breakpoint with
the rest of the group stopped: the trap flag would retire a second instruction
after a system call and a single iteration of a string loop. Breakpoint removal
puts the original bytes back only where the engine's byte is still in place, so
code the program rewrote while it ran is not overwritten.

A forked child keeps the engine's breakpoint byte in its own copy of the
memory. A trap at an address the engine recently wrote, in a task of the same
run with another address space, is repaired in the child and resumed instead of
raising SIGTRAP.

REGS_READ accepts a mask of register sets and answers them in one reply. While a
session exists the control loop polls at the next tick instead of every 10 ms.
CAPS gains SIGNALS and BATCH_REGS; STATUS and the thread list carry the signal.
Contract revision stays 17.
---
